changes.adapted_text:
On the three-dimensional torus $\mathbb T^3$, represented by the cube $[-1/2,1/2]^3$ with opposite faces identified, the velocity $u$ and pressure $p$ of an incompressible fluid satisfy the unforced Euler equations

$$\begin{equation}
 \partial_tu+(u\cdot\nabla)u+\nabla p=0,
 \qquad \operatorname{div}u=0
 \quad\hbox{on }\mathbb T^3\times[0,T_*). \tag{1.1}
\end{equation}$$ For smooth divergence-free initial data the local theory gives a unique smooth solution on a maximal interval $[0,T_*)$ \[[13](#cite-EbinMarsden), §15.2(i),(iii), pp. 150–151\]. We construct odd initial data for which $T_*<\infty$. Oddness fixes the particle at the origin, where its velocity is always zero. The vorticity $\omega=\operatorname{curl}u$ at that particle grows without bound even as the velocity converges uniformly on the entire torus.

Consider first a flow on $\mathbb R^3$ of the form $U(t,x)=D(t)x$. It solves the Euler equations if the smooth matrix $D$ has trace zero and $D'+D^2$ is symmetric. Indeed, the acceleration is $(D'+D^2)x$, the negative gradient of $p_D=-x\cdot(D'+D^2)x/2$. Craik and Criminale \[[12](#cite-CraikCriminale), §2, (2.4)–(2.10)\] superposed an exact plane wave on such flows. In the inviscid case its velocity takes the form $$w(t,x)=\mathbf a(t)F(k(t)\cdot x),\qquad \mathbf a\cdot k=0.$$ Here $F$ is smooth, odd and $1$-periodic, with $F'(0)=1$, for example $F(s)=\sin(2\pi s)/(2\pi)$. At any one time the planes $k\cdot x=s$ carry a common velocity $\mathbf a F(s)$, tangent to each plane. A displacement of length $1/|k|$ in the direction $k/|k|$ advances the phase by one period. Thus the speed is at most $|\mathbf a|\|F\|_\infty$, whereas the velocity gradient at the origin is $\mathbf a\otimes k$.

The wave advects no part of its own velocity, since motion along $\mathbf a$ leaves the phase unchanged. Transporting the phase by $Dx$ and balancing the remaining acceleration against the pressure gives $$\begin{equation}
 k'=-D^Tk,\qquad
 \mathbf a'=-D\mathbf a+p_{\rm w} k,\qquad
 p_{\rm w}=\frac{2k\cdot D\mathbf a}{|k|^2}. \tag{1.4}
\end{equation}$$ Differentiating $k\cdot\mathbf a=0$ gives the pressure coefficient. With $P'=F$ the wave pressure is $-p_{\rm w}P(k\cdot x)$, and $Dx+w$ is an exact Euler velocity. The pressure force is parallel to $k$, so it does no instantaneous work on the amplitude. Thus $\tfrac12(d/dt)|\mathbf a|^2=-\mathbf a\cdot D\mathbf a$. The pressure force turns the amplitude, maintaining its orthogonality to the wave vector. For related instabilities of planar flows see Pierrehumbert \[[35](#cite-Pierrehumbert1986)\] and Bayly \[[36](#cite-Bayly1986)\].

changes.authors_text:
We first describe the wave construction of Craik and Criminale \[[12](#cite-CraikCriminale)\]. Let $D(t)$ be a smooth matrix with $\operatorname{tr}D=0$ and $D'+D^2$ symmetric. These conditions ensure that $U(t,x)=D(t)x$, linear in space, solves the Euler equations on $\mathbb R^3$, with pressure $p_D(t,x)=-x\cdot(D'+D^2)x/2$. We add to this linear background a velocity $$w(t,x)=\mathbf a(t)F(k(t)\cdot x),\qquad \mathbf a\cdot k=0,$$ where $k(t)\ne0$ is the wave vector, $\mathbf a(t)$ is the amplitude vector, and $F$ is a smooth odd $1$-periodic profile of the scalar phase $s$. For example, one may take $F(s)=\sin(2\pi s)$. The vector $\mathbf a$ records both size and direction: the velocity amplitude is $|\mathbf a|\|F\|_\infty$, and $\mathbf a/|\mathbf a|$ is its unit direction when $\mathbf a\ne0$. The unit wave vector is $k/|k|$. Since $F$ has period one, $w$ is periodic in the direction of $k$ with period $1/|k|$, so $|k|$ is the spatial frequency of the wave.

The sum $u=Dx+w$ is an exact solution of the Euler equations if $k$ and $\mathbf a$ solve $$\begin{equation}
 k'=-D^Tk,\qquad
 \mathbf a'=-D\mathbf a+p_{\rm w} k,\qquad
 p_{\rm w}=\frac{2k\cdot D\mathbf a}{|k|^2}. \tag{1.4}
\end{equation}$$ Its pressure is $p=p_D-p_{\rm w} P(k\cdot x)$, where $P'=F$. Indeed, $\mathbf a\cdot k=0$ gives $(w\cdot\nabla)w=0$, the first ordinary differential equation transports the phase by $Dx$, and the second cancels the remaining acceleration with the gradient of the wave pressure $-p_{\rm w}P(k\cdot x)$. The displayed value of $p_{\rm w}$ preserves $\mathbf a\cdot k=0$. We call (1.4) the Craik–Criminale equations for the wave vector and the amplitude vector. Normalizing $F'(0)=1$ gives $\nabla w(t,0)=\mathbf a\otimes k$. Related short-wave instabilities of planar vortices were studied by Pierrehumbert \[[35](#cite-Pierrehumbert1986)\] and Bayly \[[36](#cite-Bayly1986)\].

changes.adapted_text:
Let $X(t,a)$ be the flow of a smooth Euler solution $u,p$ on $\mathbb T^3\times[0,T]$, so $X_t=u(t,X)$ and $X(0,a)=a$. For a smooth divergence-free correction $b$, let $X_\delta$ be the flow of $u+\delta b$ from the same labels. Write $X_\delta=X+\delta\xi+O(\delta^2)$. Differentiating its flow equation at $\delta=0$ gives

$$\begin{equation}
 \partial_t\xi(t,a)=\nabla u(t,X(t,a))\xi(t,a)+b(t,X(t,a)),
 \qquad \xi(0,a)=0. \tag{1.5}
\end{equation}$$ All particles start at the same positions, but $\xi_t(0,a)=b(0,a)$ is free. Imposing $\xi(T,a)=0$ selects this initial velocity.

changes.authors_text:
Let $u,p$ solve the Euler equations on $[0,T]$. The flow $X(t,a)$ of $u$ gives the position at time $t$ of the particle that starts at $a$. It satisfies $$\partial_tX(t,a)=u(t,X(t,a)),\qquad X(0,a)=a.$$ For a divergence-free velocity correction $b$, let $X_\delta$ be the flow of $u+\delta b$ with the same initial positions, so that $$\partial_tX_\delta(t,a)
       =(u+\delta b)(t,X_\delta(t,a)),\qquad X_\delta(0,a)=a.$$ The first-order change $\xi$ in the particle trajectories is defined by $$X_\delta(t,a)=X(t,a)+\delta\xi(t,a)+O(\delta^2).$$ Differentiating the flow equation at $\delta=0$ gives $$\begin{equation}
 \partial_t\xi(t,a)=\nabla u(t,X(t,a))\xi(t,a)+b(t,X(t,a)),
 \qquad \xi(0,a)=0. \tag{1.5}
\end{equation}$$ The initial condition follows from the common starting positions. Since also $X(0,a)=a$, evaluating (1.5) at $t=0$ gives $\partial_t\xi(0,a)=b(0,a)$. This identity holds for every $b$ and imposes no condition. We impose instead the endpoint condition $\xi(T,a)=0$. It states that $X_\delta(T,a)=X(T,a)+O(\delta^2)$: the corrected flow carries each particle to its original final position, up to an error of order $\delta^2$. When $b$ solves the linearized Euler equations (1.6) below, it is determined by $b(0)$ and the source. Under the bounds $H\le\operatorname{Id}$ and $T\le1$ below, the endpoint condition selects $b(0)$ uniquely.

changes.adapted_text:
With force $f$ the velocity correction $b$ and pressure variation $q$ satisfy

$$\begin{equation}
 \partial_t b+(u\cdot\nabla)b+(b\cdot\nabla)u+\nabla q=f,
 \qquad \operatorname{div}b=0. \tag{1.6}
\end{equation}$$ For the first mean correction $f$ cancels the parent residual and the phase average of the wave error. Differentiating (1.5) along a particle and substituting (1.6) gives the displacement equation

$$\begin{equation}
 \begin{gathered}
 \xi_{tt}+H\xi+(\nabla q)\circ X=f\circ X,
 \qquad H=(D_x^2p)\circ X,\\
 \operatorname{div}_x(\xi\circ X^{-1})=0,\qquad \xi(0)=\xi(T)=0.
 \end{gathered} \tag{1.7}
\end{equation}$$ The bound $H\le\operatorname{Id}$ means $z\cdot H(t,a)z\le|z|^2$ at every label and time. The divergence condition expresses volume preservation for the displacement in physical coordinates. For $f=0$ this is the Jacobi equation in Arnold’s geodesic description of Euler flow \[[5](#cite-Arnold1966), Section 10\].

changes.authors_text:
For a prescribed momentum source $f$, the inhomogeneous problem is to find $b,q$ satisfying the linearized Euler equations $$\begin{equation}
 \partial_t b+(u\cdot\nabla)b+(b\cdot\nabla)u+\nabla q=f,
 \qquad \operatorname{div}b=0. \tag{1.6}
\end{equation}$$ Here $q$ is the pressure variation, and the displacement induced by $b$ must satisfy $\xi(0)=\xi(T)=0$. Differentiating (1.5) in time and using (1.6) gives $$\begin{equation}
 \begin{gathered}
 \xi_{tt}+H\xi+(\nabla q)\circ X=f\circ X,
 \qquad H=(D_x^2p)\circ X,\\
 \operatorname{div}_x(\xi\circ X^{-1})=0,\qquad \xi(0)=\xi(T)=0.
 \end{gathered} \tag{1.7}
\end{equation}$$ The homogeneous equation is the Jacobi equation along the geodesic associated with this solution in Arnold’s formulation \[[5](#cite-Arnold1966)\]. The constraint $\operatorname{div}_x(\xi\circ X^{-1})=0$ expresses volume preservation to first order.

changes.adapted_text:
For a solution of (1.7), testing with $-\xi$ and integrating by parts gives the identity $$\begin{equation}
 \underbrace{\int_0^T\!\int_{\mathbb T^3}
   \bigl(|\xi_t|^2-\xi\cdot H\xi\bigr)\,\,\mathrm da\,\,\mathrm dt}_{\mathcal Q(\xi)}
 =-\int_0^T\!\int_{\mathbb T^3}(f\circ X)\cdot\xi\,\,\mathrm da\,\,\mathrm dt. \tag{1.8}
\end{equation}$$ Indeed, changing variables $x=X(t,a)$ changes the pressure pairing to $\int_{\mathbb T^3}\nabla q\cdot(\xi\circ X^{-1})\,\,\mathrm dx=0$. On volume-preserving paths with both endpoint maps fixed, $\mathcal Q$ is the second variation of the kinetic action. The family $X_\delta$ above identifies its tangent displacement $\xi$ and fixes the terminal map to first order.

If $H\le\operatorname{Id}$ and $T\le1$, then $|\xi(t,a)|^2\le(T-t)\int_t^T|\xi_\tau(\tau,a)|^2\,\,\mathrm d\tau$ gives $$\begin{equation}
 \mathcal Q(\xi)\ge\Bigl(1-\frac{T^2}{2}\Bigr)\|\xi_t\|_{L^2_{t,a}}^2
 \ge\frac12\|\xi_t\|_{L^2_{t,a}}^2 . \tag{1.9}
\end{equation}$$ A negative eigenvalue of $H$ improves the energy in its eigendirection. For example, take $y''-\mu^2y=f$ with $\mu>0$. The solution $e^{\mu t}$ grows in the homogeneous initial-value problem, while testing on paths with $y(0)=y(T)=0$ gives the positive form $\int_0^T(|y'|^2+\mu^2|y|^2)\,\,\mathrm dt$. For Euler the pressure constraint couples the particles, but the upper bound on $H$ still suffices for (1.9). With $f=0$ the energy identity forces $\xi_t=0$, hence $\xi=0$ and $b=0$ by (1.5). Thus the endpoint map $b(0)\mapsto\xi(T)$ is injective. Existence with the norms used in the iteration is the content of Theorem 4.2.

Brenier used a Dirichlet Poincaré inequality for paths with matching endpoints and an upper pressure-Hessian bound to obtain a criterion for action minimization \[[32](#cite-Brenier1989), Theorem 5.1 and Lemma 5.2, pp. 242–244\]. See also \[[33](#cite-Brenier1999), Theorem 2.4, author version\].

changes.authors_text:
For a solution of (1.7), testing with $-\xi$ and integrating by parts gives the identity $$\begin{equation}
 \underbrace{\int_0^T\!\int_{\mathbb T^3}
   \bigl(|\xi_t|^2-\xi\cdot H\xi\bigr)\,\,\mathrm da\,\,\mathrm dt}_{\mathcal Q(\xi)}
 =-\int_0^T\!\int_{\mathbb T^3}(f\circ X)\cdot\xi\,\,\mathrm da\,\,\mathrm dt. \tag{1.8}
\end{equation}$$ Volume preservation and the divergence constraint remove the pressure variation. The quadratic form $\mathcal Q$ is the fixed-endpoint second variation of the action. If $H\le\operatorname{Id}$ and $T\le1$, then ${|\xi(t,a)|^2\le(T-t)\int_t^T|\xi_\tau(\tau,a)|^2\,\,\mathrm d\tau}$ gives $$\begin{equation}
 \mathcal Q(\xi)\ge\Bigl(1-\frac{T^2}{2}\Bigr)\|\xi_t\|_{L^2_{t,a}}^2
 \ge\frac12\|\xi_t\|_{L^2_{t,a}}^2 . \tag{1.9}
\end{equation}$$ The term $-\xi\cdot H\xi$ lowers the integrand where $\xi\cdot H\xi>0$ and raises it where $\xi\cdot H\xi<0$. Thus an upper bound for $H$ gives the lower bound in (1.9). For $f=0$, (1.8) gives $\mathcal Q(\xi)=0$. Then (1.9) forces $\xi_t=0$, and $\xi(0)=0$ gives $\xi=0$. Equation (1.5) gives $b=0$, proving that the linearized endpoint map $b(0)\mapsto\xi(T)$ has trivial kernel. Upper bounds on the pressure Hessian also enter Brenier’s fixed-endpoint action-minimization criterion \[[32](#cite-Brenier1989), [33](#cite-Brenier1999)\].

For $F(s)=\sin(2\pi s)$, the ratio $F'(1/2)/F'(0)$ is $-1$. The sine profile with slope one at the origin therefore violates the lower bound in (1.10) for small $h$. Subsection 3.2 constructs $F_h$ with a narrow positive bump in $F_h'$ and a shallow negative part, as shown in Figure 2.

changes.adapted_text:
A finite step gives an approximate velocity $U$ with pressure $p_U$ and residual $R_U=\partial_tU+(U\cdot\nabla)U+\nabla p_U$. Along its flow $X$ the displacement coefficient becomes $H^\#=(D_x^2p_U-\nabla R_U)\circ X$ (Section 4). Its quadratic form sees only its symmetric part. For $0<T\le1$ and $H^\#\le\operatorname{Id}$ in this sense, Theorem 4.2 constructs a smooth correction for every smooth force. Appendix A bounds the inverse in spatial Hölder norms by a fixed power of the parent’s deformation and coefficient norms. A large negative pressure curvature is allowed, but at each step we must control the positive curvature added by the wave and the gradient of the residual left behind.

In the affine wave of Subsection 1.1, the Hessian of $-p_{\rm w}P(k\cdot x)$ is $-p_{\rm w}F'(k\cdot x)k\otimes k$. The coupling ratio $\varepsilon$ controls how the wave turns and grows. We now choose a different parameter $h$, the width in phase of the steep part of an odd periodic profile $F_h$. We require $$\begin{equation}
 F_h'(0)=1,\qquad -Ch\le F_h'(s)\le1 . \tag{1.10}
\end{equation}$$ The unit slope retains the full shear $\mathbf a\otimes k$ at the origin. In the normal direction $k/|k|$ the wave pressure has curvature $-p_{\rm w}|k|^2F_h'(k\cdot x)$, and it has zero curvature in the tangent directions. When $p_{\rm w}>0$ its large negative curvature at phase zero helps the displacement estimate. Positive curvature can arise only where $F_h'<0$, and is bounded by $$\begin{equation}
 p_{\rm w}|k|^2\bigl(-F_h'(k\cdot x)\bigr)_+
 \le Ch\,p_{\rm w}|k|^2, \tag{1.11}
\end{equation}$$ where $(r)_+=\max\{r,0\}$. Indeed $p_{\rm w}|k|^2
=2\nu_+|\mathbf a|\,|k|$. At the origin the normal curvature is $-2\nu_+$ times the shear strength, whereas the positive curvature anywhere in this affine wave is at most $2Ch\nu_+|\mathbf a|\,|k|$. Over a period the derivative of $F_h$ must have mean zero. The normalized sine profile has slope $1$ at zero and $-1$ at phase $1/2$. We instead concentrate the positive slope in a narrow interval of width $O(h)$. A shallow negative slope over the rest of the period balances its integral (Figure 2). This also gives $\|F_h\|_\infty=O(h)$, so the wave velocity is $O(h|\mathbf a|)$.

changes.authors_text:
For an approximate parent $U$ with residual $R_U$, Subsection 5.1 uses $H^\#=(D_x^2p_U-\nabla R_U)\circ X$ in place of $H$, under the same bounds $H^\#\le\operatorname{Id}$ and $T\le1$. Subsection 4.3 uses the resulting coercivity to construct a bounded inverse for the forced Jacobi problem, and Appendix A establishes the quantitative Hölder bounds of Theorem 4.2. The inverse exists on every interval $[0,T]$ with $0<T\le1$ on which $H^\#\le\operatorname{Id}$, with a bound that depends on the parent but requires no further smallness of $T$. Each correction therefore solves a linear two-point boundary value problem along the flow of the parent, and no short-time argument is used.

In the affine wave of Subsection 1.1, the Hessian of $-p_{\rm w}P(k\cdot x)$ is $-p_{\rm w}F'(k\cdot x)k\otimes k$. We seek an odd periodic profile $F_h$ satisfying $$\begin{equation}
 F_h'(0)=1,\qquad -Ch\le F_h'(s)\le1 . \tag{1.10}
\end{equation}$$ The first condition preserves the wave gradient $\mathbf a\otimes k$ at the origin, and hence the amplification described in Subsection 1.1. The lower bound on $F_h'$ controls the largest eigenvalue of $D_x^2[-p_{\rm w}P_h(k\cdot x)]$, where $P_h'=F_h$. On an interval with $p_{\rm w}>0$, its positive part satisfies $$\begin{equation}
 p_{\rm w}|k|^2\bigl(-F_h'(k\cdot x)\bigr)_+
 \le Ch\,p_{\rm w}|k|^2, \tag{1.11}
\end{equation}$$ where $(r)_+=\max\{r,0\}$. For fixed wave coefficients, taking $h$ small makes this positive part small at every point. In the construction $h$ is a negative power of the frequency of the wave. As $h$ shrinks, the wave produces thin layers of strong shear while its velocity amplitude is $O(h|\mathbf a|)$. Its gradient at the origin remains $\mathbf a\otimes k$, preserving the local shear that amplifies the next wave. The pressure Hessian added at each step is bounded above by a small multiple of the identity. Section 7 sums these bounds and controls $\|\nabla R_U\|_\infty$ to obtain $H^\#\le\operatorname{Id}$ as a quadratic form.

changes.adapted_text:
For the localized wave the pressure coefficient remains positive along the central trajectory throughout amplification. Estimate (5.19) extends this sign to the small envelope, where the spatial cutoff is nonnegative. The unit slope at zero, the small height of the profile and the shallow negative slope give three different powers of the frequency. Normalize the maximum central amplitude on the wave’s interval to $\lambda^{-3/4}$, where $\lambda$ is the length of the normal at launch, and take $h=\lambda^{-1/2}$. These choices give the iteration three simultaneous bounds. The leading velocity is $O(\lambda^{-5/4})$. At a time when the cutoff is one, the central shear has size $$|\mathbf a(t,0)|\,|\kappa(t,0)|=
 \lambda^{1/4}
 \frac{|\mathbf a(t,0)|}{\max |\mathbf a(\cdot,0)|}
 \frac{|\kappa(t,0)|}{\lambda},$$ where $\kappa$ is the transported normal. Proposition 2.8 controls the product of the amplitude ratio and the normal stretch at the successor’s launch. Up to a fixed power of the parent bound, the coefficient of $-F_h'$ in the leading pressure curvature is at most $C\lambda^{1/4}$. With the same parent factor the positive curvature is at most $C\lambda^{-1/4}$ (Lemma 5.9). Section 7 sums these upper bounds and estimates $\|\nabla R_U\|_\infty$ to keep $H^\#\le\operatorname{Id}$ at successive steps.

These powers describe this choice of amplitude and profile. The steep part of the profile has phase width $O(h)$. At the origin at launch its physical thickness is of order $h/\lambda=\lambda^{-3/2}$. Relative to the velocity bound, one spatial derivative costs $\lambda^{3/2}$, up to factors from the parent flow. Interpolating the bounds for the velocity and its first derivative gives the power $\lambda^{-5/4+3\alpha/2}$ in $C^\alpha$. Its exponent is negative precisely for $\alpha<5/6$. Section 8.4 handles the parent factors and sums the increments for each such $\alpha$ along the same sequence.

changes.authors_text:
The velocity added to $U$ is a finite expansion $$\sum_{p=1}^PZ_p+C_{P+1},\qquad Z_p=V_p+C_p+B_p,$$ where $V_p(t,x,s)$ is a profile perpendicular to $\kappa$ with zero phase average, $C_{p+1}$ is the divergence correction of $V_p$, and $B_p(t,x)$ is a mean, independent of the phase; the profiles are evaluated at $s=\theta$. The leading term is the wave, $Z_1=V_1=\widetilde\chi A_1$, with $C_1=0$ and $B_1=0$. We call $p$ the *level* of $Z_p$ and of each of $V_p$, $C_p$ and $B_p$: up to a fixed power of a number that bounds the parent, the bound for $Z_p$ is that of the wave times $\lambda^{-2(p-1)/3}$, where $\lambda$ is the frequency of the wave. The corrections are computed one level at a time up to the depth $P$, which is fixed in Section 7. The error left by the wave and its divergence correction $C_2$ is a profile $H_2(t,x,s)$ of level two. We split $H_2$ into its phase average $\langle H_2\rangle_s$ and its oscillatory part $H_2-\langle H_2\rangle_s$, as in the expansions of Cheverry \[[34](#cite-Cheverry2006), Section 4.1\].

With $B_2$ determined, the oscillatory correction $V_2$ and its pressure $\pi_2$ solve $$\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
 D_tV_2+(\nabla U)V_2+\kappa\partial_s\pi_2
   &=-(H_2-\langle H_2\rangle_s)-(B_2\cdot\kappa)\partial_s(\widetilde\chi A_1),\\
 \kappa\cdot V_2&=0,\qquad \langle V_2\rangle_s=0.
 \end{split} \tag{1.12}
\end{equation}$$ The mean $B_2$ transports the leading wave’s fast phase. This term has zero phase average and, like the oscillatory part $H_2-\langle H_2\rangle_s$, belongs to level two, so we solve for the mean $B_2$ before the oscillatory correction $V_2$. At fixed phase along each trajectory, (1.12) is a forced Craik–Criminale amplitude equation. Every wave uses zero initial data for this correction. The curl construction of Subsection 3.1 gives the divergence correction $C_3$ of $V_2$, so that $V_2+C_3$ is exactly divergence free. The term $C_3$ belongs to level three, where we solve for the mean and oscillatory corrections again.

We stop the expansion at the depth $P$, keeping the divergence correction $C_{P+1}$ of $V_P$. The remainder then has a small factor $\lambda^{-2P/3}$, once $\lambda$ is larger than a fixed power of a number that bounds the parent (Theorem 5.6). The truncated parent, localized wave and linear corrections give an approximate solution $V$ of the Euler equations. We truncate $V$ in frequency. Three choices make the three errors of the step smaller than a fixed negative power of the frequency of the next wave. The depth $P$ controls the remainder of the expansion. The frequency cutoff controls the truncation error. The ratio $\varepsilon$ of Subsection 1.1 sets the gain, which controls the error of the cutoff in time. This cutoff acts while the wave’s amplitude is at most a fixed multiple of $\exp(-\varepsilon^{-1/2})$ times its peak. These three errors make up the residual of the truncated velocity, which is the parent of the next step (Proposition 5.11). The pressure and residual-gradient estimates give coercivity for the mean equation of the next step. The leading wave and oscillatory corrections stay in the transported cutoff region. The mean corrections and the truncation difference may extend over the torus. Figure 3 summarizes the step.

changes.adapted_text:
With $B_2$ determined, the oscillatory correction $V_2$ and its pressure $\pi_2$ solve $$\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
 D_tV_2+(\nabla U)V_2+\kappa\partial_s\pi_2
   &=-(H_2-\langle H_2\rangle_s)-(B_2\cdot\kappa)\partial_s(\widetilde\chi A_1),\\
 \kappa\cdot V_2&=0,\qquad \langle V_2\rangle_s=0.
 \end{split} \tag{1.12}
\end{equation}$$ Throughout the step the phase follows $U$. The additional transport of the leading wave by $B_2$ therefore appears on the right as $-(B_2\cdot\kappa)\partial_s(\widetilde\chi A_1)$. This term has zero phase average because it differentiates a periodic function in $s$. At fixed phase along each trajectory, (1.12) is a forced Craik–Criminale amplitude equation. For each label $a$ and phase $s$, this equation determines $V_2(t,X(t,a),s)$ from zero initial data. The leading amplitude $\mathbf a$ starts from the nonzero, tilted direction of Subsection 1.1 for every wave after the first. Perpendicularity to $\kappa$ cancels the fast part of the divergence, leaving the spatial derivatives at fixed phase. The curl construction of Subsection 3.1 gives the divergence correction $C_3$ of $V_2$, so that $V_2+C_3$ is exactly divergence free. The term $C_3$ belongs to level three, where we solve for the mean and oscillatory corrections again.

In the whole-space construction of OpenAI \[[56](#cite-OpenAIEuler2026), §§3.3–3.4, (3.19), (3.27)–(3.28)\], a condition on the initial mean velocity makes it compactly supported. For waves activated at $t_0>0$, the forced transverse correction there has zero displacement at both ends of $[0,t_0]$. Here transverse corrections start with zero velocity at every step. The stages in the whole-space construction are exact Euler solutions, and the limiting datum has maximal lifespan at most their accumulation time \[[56](#cite-OpenAIEuler2026), §6.2\]. Here the approximate periodic flows converge in every spatial $C^m$ norm uniformly on each earlier interval, and the central vorticity identifies the accumulation time with the maximal lifespan.

At wave $j$ the expansion stops after a finite number $P$ of levels, retaining $C_{P+1}$ to complete the last oscillation. Theorem 5.6 gains $\lambda^{-2/3}$ per level up to parent factors. The residual also contains the derivative of the time cutoff, supported while the wave is exponentially small relative to its peak. Finally the corrected velocity is truncated in Fourier space. The depth, gain and truncation frequency make the combined residual small enough for wave $j+1$ (Proposition 5.11).

The oscillation vanishes up to its launch and is thereafter confined to a shrinking neighborhood of the origin before Fourier truncation. The phase-independent correction and the Fourier tail may already be present at time zero. Figure 4 shows why only the latter terms need to be summed in high spatial norms on any closed interval preceding the accumulation time.

One sequence must converge through every fixed derivative order. At step $j$ let $\lambda_j$ denote the wave number at launch, $q_j$ the order to which the error is controlled, and $\Lambda_j$ the Fourier cutoff after correction. We keep $q_j$ fixed until the preceding frequency can absorb the constants at order $q_j+1$. The frequencies grow without bound, so every fixed order is eventually reached.

On a first reading the two facts to retain are that $q_j\to\infty$ and that the future logarithmic gain satisfies the bound below. Section 7 uses the full recursion. The frequencies and target ratios are fixed in advance. Each launch after the first two is timed by a crossing of the prescribed ratio.

With the smooth profiles fixed, let $b$ be the exponent in Subsection 5.6 and put $K(s)=320b(s+1)$. Choose a nondecreasing threshold $\mathcal B(s)$ large enough for all the estimates through order and depth $7K(s)$ specified in Appendix C. We also require $$\begin{equation}
 \log\mathcal B(s)\ge4K(s+1)^3\qquad(s\ge1). \tag{1.13}
\end{equation}$$

changes.authors_text:
Fix the smooth profiles used in the construction. The integer $b\ge1$ is the fixed integer specified in Subsection 5.6. For integers $s\ge1$ put $K(s)=320b(s+1)$. The exponent $K$ separates successive frequencies.

The estimates of Sections 3, 5, 6 and 7 and of Lemma B.1 contain constants that depend only on an order of differentiation, the depth of the expansion and the fixed profiles. We call them the fixed-order constants. Choose a nondecreasing function $\mathcal B$ on the positive integers such that $\mathcal B(s)$ bounds the fixed-order constants at all orders and depths at most $7K(s)$, and $$\begin{equation}
 \log\mathcal B(s)\ge4K(s+1)^3\qquad(s\ge1). \tag{1.13}
\end{equation}$$

changes.adapted_text:
Choose the initial frequency parameter $N\ge\mathcal B(1)$ and start at $q_1=1$. The recursion reads

$$\begin{equation}
 \begin{gathered}
 K_j=K(q_j),\qquad
 \lambda_0=N,\qquad \lambda_j=\lambda_{j-1}^{K_j}\quad(j\ge1),\qquad
 \Lambda_j=\lambda_j^5\quad(j\ge0),\\
 q_{j+1}=\max\{1\le s\le q_j+1:\ \mathcal B(s)\le\lambda_{j-1}\}\quad(j\ge1),\\
 L_j=4\log\lambda_{j+1},\qquad
 \varepsilon_{j-1}=L_j^{-2}
 \quad(j\ge2).
 \end{gathered} \tag{1.14}
\end{equation}$$ The stationary shear has cutoff $\Lambda_0$. For $j\ge2$, $L_j$ measures the logarithmic amplification needed to keep the wave small while its time cutoff changes. By (2.24) its amplitude then is at most $\exp(3-L_j)$ times its maximum on the comparison interval. The following bound keeps a later logarithmic gain small compared with powers of the frequency already available.

