An absolute error in amplitude can grow as rapidly as the wave itself. To control its direction over the whole interval, divide out the reference growth and use the reference solution as one coordinate direction. Its second component $a_{\rm ref}$ is positive throughout the interval. Write $\alpha_2=a_{\rm ref}h$ and $\alpha_1=-a_{\rm ref}(Yh+l\cos\varphi)$. The term in $h$ follows the reference direction, and the term in $l$ measures the departure from it. Here $h$ denotes the relative amplitude. The profile width of Section 3 enters only after the wave comparison. Inverting gives $$\begin{equation}
 h=\frac{\alpha_2}{a_{\rm ref}},\qquad
 l=-\frac{\alpha_1+Y\alpha_2}{a_{\rm ref}\cos\varphi} \tag{2.15}
\end{equation}$$ for the *normalized coordinates* of $(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)$. These are linear in the two components even when $\alpha_2=0$. The factor $1/\cos\varphi$ in $l$ gives the equations for $h$ and $l$ below the same factor $\cos\varphi$, which yields a propagator bound uniform in $\varepsilon$ and in the length of the interval. By the second equation of (2.5) and $a_{\rm ref}'=Ya_{\rm ref}$, we have $h'=l\cos\varphi$. The trace of the reference coefficient matrix is $-\varepsilon\tau\sin2\varphi=2(\log\cos\varphi)'$. By Liouville’s formula, the determinant $$\det\begin{pmatrix}\alpha_1&-Ya_{\rm ref}\\
                      \alpha_2&a_{\rm ref}\end{pmatrix}
 =a_{\rm ref}(\alpha_1+Y\alpha_2)=-a_{\rm ref}^2l\cos\varphi$$ is a constant multiple of $\cos^2\varphi$. Formula (2.14) thus gives $$l(\tau)=l(0)\exp\left(-2\int_0^\tau(1+e)\cos\varphi\,d\tau'\right).$$ The reference system becomes $$\begin{equation}
 h'=l\cos\varphi,\qquad l'=-2(1+e)l\cos\varphi, \tag{2.16}
\end{equation}$$ and the reference solution is $(h,l)=(1,0)$. The second equation shows that $|l|$ does not increase. Since $e\ge0$, for $\tau_1\le\tau_2$, $$|h(\tau_2)-h(\tau_1)|\le\int_{\tau_1}^{\tau_2}|l|\cos\varphi
 \le\int_{\tau_1}^{\tau_2}(1+e)|l|\cos\varphi
 =\tfrac12\bigl(|l(\tau_1)|-|l(\tau_2)|\bigr).$$ The triangle inequality and $|l(\tau_2)|\le|l(\tau_1)|$ give $$|h(\tau_2)|+|l(\tau_2)|\le|h(\tau_1)|+|l(\tau_1)|.$$ Since $|(h,l)|\le|h|+|l|\le\sqrt2\,|(h,l)|$, the propagator $\Psi_0(\tau_2,\tau_1)$ of (2.16) has norm at most $2$ for $\tau_1\le\tau_2$, uniformly in both times and in $\varepsilon$.

