To estimate the strength at a crossing, we compare the amplitude there with its maximum on $[0,T]$. By (2.18), $|\mathbf a|$ lies between $\exp(-\varepsilon)a_{\rm ref}$ and $\exp(\varepsilon)a_{\rm ref}$. The maximum of $a_{\rm ref}$ on $[0,T]$ is attained at $\tau_m$, so for $\tau_m\le\tau\le T$, $|\mathbf a(\tau)|/\max_{[0,T]}|\mathbf a|$ is bounded above and below by absolute multiples of $a_{\rm ref}(\tau)/a_{\rm ref}(\tau_m)$. Formula (2.14) gives $$\frac{a_{\rm ref}(\tau)}{a_{\rm ref}(\tau_m)}
 =\left(\frac{\cos\varphi(\tau)}{\cos\varphi(\tau_m)}\right)^{1/2}
   \exp\left(\int_{\tau_m}^{\tau}(1+e)\cos\varphi\,d\tau'\right).$$ The integral lies between zero and two, since $\int_{\tau_m}^\infty\cos\varphi\,d\tau\le2/(\varepsilon\tau_m)\le1$. Since $2/\varepsilon\le\tan\varphi(\tau_m)\le9/(2\varepsilon)$, $\cos\varphi(\tau_m)$ is comparable to $\varepsilon$. At a crossing $\cos\varphi$ is comparable to $\varepsilon_+$. The preceding amplitude comparison and the normal-length estimate (2.10) now give, at the same time, $$\frac{|\mathbf a(\tau)|}{\max_{[0,T]}|\mathbf a|}
       \asymp\sqrt{\frac{\varepsilon_+}{\varepsilon}},
 \qquad |k(\tau)|\asymp\frac{\lambda}{\varepsilon_+}.$$ Here $\asymp$ denotes upper and lower bounds by absolute constant multiples. Multiplying the two estimates gives (2.23).

