Let $F_0$ be a smooth odd $1$-periodic profile and $P_0$ its zero-mean periodic primitive. The *leading oscillation* and its pressure are $$\begin{equation}
 A_1=g\mathbf a F_0,\qquad \mathsf p[A_1]=-p_{\rm w}gP_0,\qquad
 p_{\rm w}=2\frac{\kappa\cdot M\mathbf a}{|\kappa|^2}. \tag{3.3}
\end{equation}$$ Along the particle with label $a$ the leading oscillation is $A_1^\natural(t,X(t,a))=g(a)\mathbf a(t,a)F_0(\phi(a))$. Its value on this particle involves only the amplitude at $a$, but its spatial derivative also uses the neighboring labels. Componentwise the first chain rule in (3.1) gives $$\partial_{x_j}A_{1,i}^\natural
   =\bigl[\mathbf d_j(g\mathbf a)_i\,F_0+
                 (g\mathbf a)_i\,\kappa_j F_0'\bigr]^\natural .$$ Here the first term differentiates the envelope and the amplitude and the second differentiates the periodic profile. Multiplying (3.2) by $gF_0$ gives $A_{1,t}+MA_1=p_{\rm w}\kappa gF_0$, and $P_0'=F_0$ gives $$\begin{equation}
 A_{1,t}+MA_1+\kappa\partial_s\mathsf p[A_1]=0 . \tag{3.4}
\end{equation}$$

