For the wave of Subsection 3.1, take a time cutoff $\widetilde\chi$ with activation time $0\le t_0<T$ and a constant $k_*\ne0$ such that $|\kappa(t_0,0)|=\lambda\ge1$. Since $\kappa=(D_aX)^{-T}k_*$, (5.1) gives $$\begin{equation}
 \mathsf D^{-1}\lambda\le|k_*|\le\mathsf D\lambda,\qquad
 \mathsf D^{-2}\lambda\le|\kappa|\le\mathsf D^2\lambda . \tag{5.3}
\end{equation}$$ Take $F_0=F_h$ from Lemma 3.1 with $h=\lambda^{-1/2}$. At $a=0$, solve (3.2) on $[t_0,T]$ with $\mathbf a\perp\kappa$ and normalize $\max|\mathbf a(t,0)|=\lambda^{-3/4}$. A nonzero solution of the linear seed equation never vanishes, so we can put $$\gamma(t)=\lambda^{3/4}|\mathbf a(t,0)|,\qquad
 \nu(t,0)=\Bigl(\frac{\kappa}{|\kappa|}\cdot
          M\frac{\mathbf a}{|\mathbf a|}\Bigr)(t,0)
 \qquad(t_0\le t\le T).$$ The first coupling is $\nu(t,0)$ and the strength is $|\mathbf a(t,0)||\kappa(t,0)|$, as in Lemma 2.2. At other labels solve the same equation with initial data $$\begin{equation}
 \mathbf a(t_0,a)=\bigl(\operatorname{Id}-P_{\kappa(t_0,a)}\bigr)\mathbf a(t_0,0),
 \qquad P_\kappa=\frac{\kappa\otimes\kappa}{|\kappa|^2}, \tag{5.4}
\end{equation}$$ the projection of the vector at the origin onto the plane perpendicular to $\kappa(t_0,a)$. It is even in $a$.

Choose a smooth $\chi:[0,\infty)\to[0,1]$ equal to one on $[0,1]$ and zero on $[2,\infty)$. The even envelope $g(a)=\chi(|a|/\ell_*)$ keeps the seed intact for $|a|\le\ell_*$ and cuts it off for $|a|\ge2\ell_*$. Thus $A_1=g\mathbf a F_h$ in (3.3) and $V_1=\widetilde\chi A_1$ in (3.7).

At the origin, assume that for $t_0\le t'\le t\le T$, $$\begin{equation}
 \frac{|\mathbf b(t)|}{\gamma(t)}
 \le\mathsf D\,\frac{|\mathbf b(t')|}{\gamma(t')},\qquad
 \nu(t,0)\ge\mathsf D^{-1}, \tag{5.5}
\end{equation}$$ the first inequality holding for every transverse solution $\mathbf b$ at the origin. On a small enough envelope, comparison with the central solution extends both the relative growth bound and the strict sign $\kappa\cdot M\mathbf a>0$ to every label there (Lemma 5.3). Rescaling $k_*$ by a positive factor preserves the normal direction and the transverse amplitude equation. Normalizing the seed changes neither its direction nor the relative growth $\gamma(t)/\gamma(t')$. These hypotheses can therefore be checked before choosing the frequency and depth.

