changes.adapted_text:
The radius $\ell_*$ measures the envelope in *labels*. Within it, a unit of phase has local normal length $|\kappa|^{-1}$ in physical space, and the steep part of the profile has thickness $h/|\kappa|$. At launch at the origin these lengths are $\lambda^{-1}$ and $\lambda^{-3/2}$. The much finer Fourier cutoff at $\lambda^5$ will be applied to the completed velocity in Section 7. By (5.19) the seed on the envelope is at most twice its size at the origin. Multiplication by $F_h$, with $\|F_h\|_\infty\le Ch$, bounds the velocity by $C\lambda^{-5/4}$. The first mean must cancel the averaged quadratic forcing and $R_U$. Averaging turns the forcing in (5.25) into a derivative of the stress at fixed phase. This leaves the square of the velocity scale, up to parent factors, so we require $$\begin{equation}
 \left\lVert R_U\right\rVert_{C_tL^\infty}\le\lambda^{-5/2}. \tag{5.6}
\end{equation}$$ Increasing $\lambda$ makes this bound on the inherited residual more stringent. At wave $j$, item <span class="upright">(T2)</span> of Theorem 7.1 at step $j-1$ already gives $\|R_{j-1}\|_{C_tL^\infty}\le\lambda_j^{-3}$. The radius of the envelope is a negative power of $\mathsf D$ with an absolute exponent, $\ell_*\le\mathsf D^{-3}$. The exponent is fixed sufficiently large for the finitely many estimates of Subsections 5.2–5.4. In particular the support of $g$ lies in the interior of $[-1/2,1/2]^3$.

