 B_t+MB+\mathbf d\bar q=\bar f,\qquad \mathbf d\cdot B=0,\qquad
 z(0)=z(T)=0,\quad B=D_aX z_t. \tag{5.11}
\end{equation}$$ In the notation of that theorem, $\bar f=f\circ X$, $g=(D_aX)^T\bar f$, $v=z_t$, and $\bar q$ is the pressure $\mathsf q$ of (4.11). We call (5.11) the *mean equation*. The terminal condition selects the initial velocity $B(0)$, so the mean can already be present before activation. We estimate it on all of $[0,T]$ in the unweighted label norms. The inverse preserves the derivative order in the scaled Hölder norms. Passing to the supremum norms by (D.2) costs one derivative of the source. At each level of the expansion the mean source is formed from terms already estimated at all orders.

