Start from $V_1,\mathsf p_1,C_2$ in (3.7) and put $B_1=0$, so that $Y_1=Z_1=V_1$. Set aside the activation error $R_{\widetilde\chi}$ of (3.8). The terms left by the leading wave at level two are $$\begin{equation}
 H_2=\widetilde\chi\bigl[(\partial_t+M)C[A_1]+\mathbf d\mathsf p[A_1]\bigr]
   +(V_1\cdot\mathbf d)V_1+(C_2\cdot\kappa)\partial_sV_1 . \tag{5.25}
\end{equation}$$ The first mean is forced by a slow derivative of an averaged stress. The linear term has zero phase mean. In the last term we can transfer the fast derivative to the divergence correction. Indeed, (3.6) and integration over one period give $$\langle (C_2\cdot\kappa)\partial_sV_1\rangle_s
 =-\langle \partial_s(C_2\cdot\kappa)V_1\rangle_s
 =\langle (\mathbf d\cdot V_1)V_1\rangle_s.$$ Adding the other quadratic term gives the row divergence of $\langle V_1\otimes V_1\rangle_s$, as in the mean equation of Cheverry \[[34](#cite-Cheverry2006), Section 4.1, (4.19)\]. In components, $(\langle H_2\rangle_s)_\ell=\sum_i\mathbf d_i\langle (V_1)_\ell(V_1)_i\rangle_s$. Both factors have the size of $V_1$. The remaining derivative acts at fixed phase, so it costs only a power of $\mathsf D$. By (5.22) and (D.3), $\|\langle H_2\rangle_s\|_{[n]}\le C_n\mathsf D^C\lambda^{-5/2}$. The residual $R_U\circ X$ has the same bound by (5.10). We include it in the first mean equation.

