Large curl at the launches would still allow cancellation between them. Fix a large $j$ and follow the origin throughout $[t_j,t_{j+1}]$. Wave $j-1$ is fully active while wave $j$ switches on. We measure the curl in a direction in which the new wave cannot cancel its predecessor. Applying <span class="upright">(T4)</span> of Theorem 7.1 at steps $j-1$ and $j$ gives, at the origin, $$\nabla U_j=\nabla U_{j-2}
   +\mathbf a_{j-1}\otimes\kappa_{j-1}
   +\widetilde\chi_j\,\mathbf a_j\otimes\kappa_j+E_{c,j-1}+E_{c,j}.$$ A gradient $\mathbf a\otimes\kappa$ contributes $\kappa\times\mathbf a$ to vorticity. At each time we project $\operatorname{curl}U_j$ along the actual wave vector $\kappa_j(t,0)$, which removes the leading curl of wave $j$. The preceding wave contributes $\kappa_{j-1}\times\mathbf a_{j-1}=\sigma_{j-1}E_3$ in its own frame. By (2.3) and Lemma 2.4, the $E_3$ component of $\kappa_j/\lambda_j$ is at least $1/2$ and its length is at most $C(1+\tau^2)$ at normalized time $\tau$. The normalized length of $[t_j,S_j]$ is at most $CL_{j+1}^4$ by item 2 of the proof of Theorem 7.1. Thus $$\begin{equation}
 \frac{\kappa_j}{|\kappa_j|}\cdot(\kappa_{j-1}\times\mathbf a_{j-1})
    \ge c\sigma_{j-1}L_{j+1}^{-8},\qquad
 \frac{\kappa_j}{|\kappa_j|}\cdot(\kappa_j\times\mathbf a_j)=0. \tag{8.1}
\end{equation}$$

