It remains to bound the logarithmic gain at the coming stages by the frequency already available at step $j$. Taking a logarithm turns three successive frequency powers into a product of three exponents. By (1.14), $$L_{j+1}=4\log\lambda_{j+2}
        =4K_jK_{j+1}K_{j+2}\log\lambda_{j-1}.$$ The largest future order here is $q_{j+2}\le q_{j+1}+1$. The floor in (1.13) was chosen to bound this product even if both intervening steps raise the order. Indeed, $$4K_jK_{j+1}K_{j+2}\le4K(q_{j+1}+1)^3
 \le\log\mathcal B(q_{j+1})\le\log\lambda_{j-1}.$$ $\square$

